export const KNOWLEDGE_SKILL_FILES: { path: string; content: string }[] = [
  { path: "SKILL.md", content: "---\nname: rei-knowledge\ndescription: \"Write or reorganize Knowledge pages: page structure, the Markdown Rei renders, links to pages and sections, and edit operations. Load before creating a page or making any edit beyond an append.\"\n---\n\n# Knowledge pages\n\nKnowledge pages are shared Markdown that people and agents read and edit. Write each page for a reader who arrives cold, sees the outline, and jumps to one section. Formatting that renders is in `references/markdown.md` (read it with `read_skill_file({ name: \"rei-knowledge\", path: \"references/markdown.md\" })`).\n\n## Page shape\n\n- A clear title and a one-line description: browsing shows only these.\n- A short summary at the top: what the page holds and its current state.\n- One topic per page. Split when a page covers two topics, never because of length.\n- Headings make each section stand alone: a section reads correctly without the sections around it.\n\n## Editing\n\n- Read the page first; the read gives `baseRevision`.\n- Use the smallest operation that does the job: `replace_text`, then `replace_section` or `append_to_section`, then a wider rewrite.\n- On a conflict, the error returns the current text: merge your change into it and edit again, keeping the other writer's changes.\n- `append` and `set_task` need no base revision.\n- A locked page allows `append` only.\n\n## Linking\n\n- `[text](page:<id>)` links a page; `[text](file:<id>)` links a library file at its current version.\n- `[text](page:<id>#<slug>)` links one section. Copy the slug from the outline `knowledge_read` returns (`§3 Week 2 #week-2`); never guess it. Renaming or removing a heading breaks links to it: the write result names those pages, so fix them.\n- `file:<id>@<versionId>` pins an exact version: use it for evidence that must not change.\n- Link a related page instead of copying its text.\n\n## Upkeep\n\n- Record decisions with their reasons and a date.\n- Update a page that has gone stale instead of writing a second one beside it.\n- Archive is reversible: archive pages that no longer apply.\n- Tags are flat labels shared by everyone. Reuse an existing spelling.\n- A page written after reading outside content carries an \"Outside content\" badge: weigh it accordingly.\n" },
  { path: "references/markdown.md", content: "# Markdown Rei renders\n\nEvery example below renders as structure. Anything else shows as plain text.\n\n## Headings and text\n\n````markdown\n## Heading\nPlain text with **strong**, *emphasis*, ~~struck~~ and `code`.\n````\n\n## Lists and tasks\n\nA task item starts with `[ ]` or `[x]`. `@agent` mentions an agent and `📅 YYYY-MM-DD` sets a due date; both render as chips in task items only.\n\n````markdown\n- first point\n- second point\n\n1. step one\n2. step two\n\n- [ ] Check the numbers @researcher 📅 2026-12-01\n- [x] Draft the summary\n````\n\n## Tables\n\n````markdown\n| Option | Cost |\n|:-------|-----:|\n| A      | 10   |\n| B      | 12   |\n````\n\n## Code\n\nAdd a language after the fence so the code is highlighted.\n\n`````markdown\n```ts\nconst total = items.length;\n```\n`````\n\nA `mermaid` fence is drawn as a diagram (flowcharts, sequence diagrams and so on); if it has a syntax error the reader sees the code instead, so keep it simple.\n\n`````markdown\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n  A[Idea] --> B[Page]\n```\n`````\n\n## Callouts and toggles\n\nKinds: NOTE, TIP, IMPORTANT, WARNING, CAUTION. A trailing `-` makes a toggle that starts closed, `+` one that starts open.\n\n````markdown\n> [!NOTE] Decision\n> We chose option A because it costs less.\n\n> [!TIP]-\n> A closed toggle for detail most readers skip.\n````\n\n## Links and images\n\n````markdown\nSee [the plan](page:pg_example) and [its second week](page:pg_example#week-2).\nRead [the report](file:fl_example), or [the report as reviewed](file:fl_example@v_example) for pinned evidence.\n![Chart](file:fl_example)\n````\n\nA section's slug is its heading in lowercase, without punctuation except `-` and `_`, with spaces as `-`; letters of any script stay (`## 第二週 進度` is `#第二週-進度`). A repeated heading gets `-1`, `-2` in page order. `knowledge_read` shows each slug in the outline. A link to a slug the page does not have opens the page at the top. File links take no `#`.\n\nImages render only from the file library (`![alt](file:<id>)`). Any other image URL shows as a link. Only `http:`, `https:` and `mailto:` links work.\n\n## Footnotes\n\nUse footnotes for sources: put an `http` or `file:` link inside the note so the reader can check it. A reference is numbered by first use; the definition starts a line (indent to continue it) and may sit anywhere, usually at the end of its section. A reference with no definition shows as plain text.\n\n````markdown\nOption A costs less.[^cost] It is also slower.[^bench]\n\n[^cost]: Vendor price list, [pricing page](https://example.com/pricing).\n[^bench]: Our run, [benchmark file](file:fl_example).\n````\n\n## Not rendered\n\nHTML shows as text. Do not use it.\n" },
];
